WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.210 --> 00:00:03.730
- Millions of Americans are
receiving stimulus checks

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:06.040
worth up to $1,400 each.

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:07.290
You might be one of them.

00:00:08.223 --> 00:00:10.490
It's all part of President
Biden's $1.9 trillion

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:11.833
American Rescue Plan.

00:00:12.763 --> 00:00:14.360
- The American Rescue
Plan is already doing

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.140
what it was designed to do,

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:19.400
make a difference in
people's everyday lives.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.120
- But you might've also heard people say

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.130
that if all that money gets spent,

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:26.220
the economy will overheat,
and we'll get inflation.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:28.210
In this video, we'll answer the question

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:30.920
of whether stimulus checks
will lead to inflation.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:34.310
Short answer, it could
but not necessarily.

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:35.670
And the reason is that the link

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:38.770
between stimulus checks
and inflation is indirect.

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:41.570
There's a lot of other things
going on at the same time.

00:00:42.512 --> 00:00:45.095
(upbeat music)

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:51.610
First of all, not all of the
checks are going to be spent.

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:53.300
Some people who didn't lose their jobs

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.170
and are doing pretty well

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:56.552
will save their money instead.

00:00:56.552 --> 00:00:58.020
You know, put it in their 401k,

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.370
or they'll use the money to
pay off their credit card bills

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:01.503
or other debts.

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:03.283
So if that money doesn't get spent

00:01:03.283 --> 00:01:06.171
then it won't generate economic activity,

00:01:06.171 --> 00:01:09.006
and it therefore will
not cause any inflation.

00:01:09.006 --> 00:01:10.490
However, some people
will spend the checks,

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:13.480
perhaps on new furniture
or a car or on a trip

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:14.313
or they could use it

00:01:14.313 --> 00:01:17.050
to pay more for their rent or groceries.

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:19.690
And remember this isn't
the only spending going on.

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:22.320
There's also billions of
dollars being sent out

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.366
by the federal government

00:01:24.366 --> 00:01:25.380
as additional unemployment insurance.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:26.510
They're also sending out money

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:28.460
for state and local governments.

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:30.840
There's money being sent
out to help small businesses

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.010
hold onto or hire workers.

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:35.550
Also, people have a lot
of savings stored up

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:37.540
from last year's stimulus bills as well,

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:39.910
and they're just waiting
for the virus to end

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:42.510
and for businesses to reopen
so that they can go out

00:01:43.429 --> 00:01:44.548
and spend that money.

00:01:44.548 --> 00:01:46.120
And when that happens,
businesses will need to go out

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:47.670
and hire back a lot of the workers

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:49.053
who got laid off last year.

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:54.340
So there'll be a lot of
spending and hiring going on,

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:56.000
not just because of the stimulus checks,

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.830
but for other reasons too.

00:01:57.830 --> 00:02:01.020
But will that be enough
to overheat the economy?

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:02.300
Let's step back for a moment

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:05.040
and ask how overheating takes place,

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:08.320
Inflation happens when the
demand for goods and services

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:10.370
runs ahead of the economy's capacity

00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:12.740
to supply those goods and services

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:14.943
with its current labor force.

00:02:16.191 --> 00:02:18.260
Think of the economy's
capacity like a container,

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:21.250
and demand is like water
going into that container.

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:24.040
If people go out and spend
their stimulus checks,

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:26.540
that starts to fill up the container.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:28.180
Businesses go out and hire workers

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:30.033
who go out and spend their paychecks.

00:02:30.908 --> 00:02:33.060
And that also helps to fill the container,

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.110
but there's millions of
workers out of a job,

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:37.520
thousands of businesses that are idle.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:39.950
So we could have lots of
spending and lots of hiring

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:42.280
without the economy
running out of capacity

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:44.243
or the container filling up with water.

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:48.193
Not until the container
overflows do you get inflation.

00:02:49.910 --> 00:02:52.773
Now you may have noticed that
the prices for some things

00:02:52.773 --> 00:02:55.160
are already going up, for
airline tickets and gasoline

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:56.240
and lumber.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.330
That's not really inflation.

00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:01.490
Inflation is when most prices
are going up, not just a few.

00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:04.540
And when they're going up year after year.

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:06.070
What you're seeing right now

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:09.130
are just one time only price
increases in industries

00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:11.203
that have specific bottlenecks.

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:17.400
So right now the stimulus checks

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:20.060
are probably not going to cause inflation.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:21.140
Here's why.

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:23.020
Remember what I said about the economy

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:24.213
being like a container?

00:03:25.171 --> 00:03:27.200
Well, the container is still
a long way from being full.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:30.240
There are more than 9 million
fewer people with jobs now

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:31.960
than before the pandemic.

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:33.840
That's a lot of slack in the labor market,

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:35.460
and it's gonna take a lot of spending,

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:38.650
a lot of hiring over many, many months

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:40.110
before we start to run out of workers.

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:41.780
Economists don't really know

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:44.020
how much capacity the economy has.

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:46.890
It's like they don't know
how big the container is.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:49.530
We might run the water long
enough to fill the container

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:51.690
and discover that the
container has stretched

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:52.663
and gotten bigger.

00:03:53.633 --> 00:03:54.851
In other words, we may think

00:03:54.851 --> 00:03:57.320
we can only get the unemployment
rate down to say 4%,

00:03:57.320 --> 00:03:59.000
and then we'll run out of workers.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:01.290
But maybe as unemployment goes down,

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:02.700
we'll find a lot more people

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:04.950
coming into the labor
force to look for jobs.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:09.340
One final point, inflation
is partly psychological.

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:12.360
Businesses are more likely
to raise prices and wages

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:14.800
if they think everyone else
will do the same thing.

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:17.670
In other words, if they
expect higher inflation,

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:19.240
they'll act in a way

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:21.240
that makes higher inflation more likely,

00:04:22.393 --> 00:04:23.480
but inflation has been low for years.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:25.100
In fact, it's too low.

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:28.840
The federal reserve targets
2%, which they think is ideal.

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:30.940
Inflation has been so low for so long

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:33.233
people will assume it will stay that way.

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:38.600
So I just explained
why the stimulus checks

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:41.020
aren't likely to produce inflation soon,

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:43.850
but maybe in a few years
we'll get inflation.

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:45.150
How would that happen?

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:48.080
Well, if we succeed in
vaccinating most of the country,

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:51.210
the virus will go away, and
most of the economy will reopen.

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:54.150
So people will start
spending again big time.

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:55.540
And maybe those stimulus checks

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:57.620
won't be the last of the stimulus.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:04:59.050
President Biden is talking

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:00.823
about a big infrastructure package.

00:05:01.931 --> 00:05:04.060
- This not only creates jobs,

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:06.820
but it makes us a hell
of a lot more competitive

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:09.414
around the world if we have
the best infrastructure

00:05:09.414 --> 00:05:10.850
in the world.

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:13.889
- So in a year or two,
unemployment could fall close to 3%

00:05:13.889 --> 00:05:15.811
for the first time in half a century.

00:05:15.811 --> 00:05:16.644
That would look like a labor market

00:05:16.644 --> 00:05:17.963
that has run out of slack,

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:21.020
like the container has
started to overflow.

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:26.400
There's one important reason

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:29.010
why we haven't had high
inflation in a long time.

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:31.460
The federal reserve didn't let it happen.

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:33.620
As soon as it thought the
economy was overheating

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:37.150
because unemployment was low,
it raised interest rates.

00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:39.880
And often that put us into a recession,

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:41.980
but Jerome Powell, the Chairman of the Fed

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:44.090
says this time is different.

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:47.400
Because inflation has
been below 2% for so long,

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:51.110
Powell wants inflation to
actually go above 2% for awhile.

00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:53.390
That's why he's keeping
interest rates at zero,

00:05:53.390 --> 00:05:55.120
and that's why he plans to keep them

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:57.870
close to zero until 2024 at least.

00:05:57.870 --> 00:05:59.270
- We're not done, and I'd hate to see us

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:00.680
take our eye off the ball

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:02.760
before we actually finish the job.

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:04.593
- There's another thing that's different.

00:06:05.549 --> 00:06:07.593
For a long time, all the
politicians and the experts

00:06:07.593 --> 00:06:09.690
said keeping inflation low
should be the priority.

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:10.950
Not any longer.

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:13.000
Now Presidents, Congress, and the Fed

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:15.530
say low unemployment especially
for disadvantaged groups

00:06:15.530 --> 00:06:16.980
is more important.

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:19.460
So even if inflation starts
to look like a problem,

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:22.730
maybe the fed will be reluctant
to raise interest rates.

00:06:22.730 --> 00:06:26.140
Also those stimulus checks,
that's not really free money.

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:27.740
The government borrowed it.

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:30.333
And as a result, the
national debt is sky high.

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:34.020
If the Fed raised interest
rates to fight inflation,

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:35.500
paying the interest on that debt

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:37.710
quickly gets very expensive.

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:40.670
So if the Fed ever decides it
needs to raise interest rates,

00:06:40.670 --> 00:06:43.540
there'll be a lot of
politicians hoping it doesn't,

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:45.573
and maybe pressuring it not to.

00:06:46.411 --> 00:06:48.994
(upbeat music)

